## Why This Is Asked
Streaming generation with chunked inputs is common in real-time chat; understanding and controlling cross-chunk attention drift is essential for reliability, especially in multilingual contexts.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming/incremental generation and chunk boundaries
- Attention drift across chunks and languages
- Boundary-aware gating vs retraining
- Lightweight diagnostic pipelines and drift metrics (KL/JS)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal example: compute per-head attention mass on current chunk
function headMass(attn, currentIdx, prevCount) {
  // attn: [heads][tokens][tokens]
  const heads = attn.length;
  const currentMass = [];
  for (let h = 0; h < heads; h++) {
    let sumAll = 0;
    let sumCurrent = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < attn[h].length; i++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < attn[h][i].length; j++) {
        sumAll += attn[h][i][j];
        if (i >= currentIdx - prevCount && i < currentIdx) {
          sumCurrent += attn[h][i][j];
        }
      }
    }
    currentMass.push(sumCurrent / (sumAll || 1));
  }
  return currentMass;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate boundary gating on real data without labeled drift? 
- What failure modes would you anticipate with multilingual code-switching?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes a candidate's ability to design production-grade AI agents that operate in finance, where data freshness, latency, and reliability are critical. It tests practical planning, tool orchestration, and robust failure handling.

## Key Concepts
- LangChain Tool integration and agent loops
- Autogen-driven hierarchical planning with conditional branches
- Tool orchestration across data fetch, risk modeling, analytics, and alerts
- Data freshness, latency handling, timeouts, and retries
- Idempotence, caching (Redis), and auditable decision logs

## Code Example
```javascript
